faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
The outage is not the WAN uplink or R1. IP reachability to the “Internet” is fine: PC1 can ping outside IPs (e.g., 203.0.113.1/.5), R1 can ping its ISP neighbor (203.0.113.1), and the core to R1 links are up. The failure users see is DNS resolution. PC1 is configured to use 10.5.0.4 as DNS (srv1). Queries to it are refused with “recursion not available” (nslookup from PC1 shows REFUSED). On srv1, BIND is running and its configuration explicitly disables recursion and only serves an internal authoritative zone: named.conf contains “recursion no; … zone ‘jeremysitlab.com’ { type master; }”. Therefore, any lookup for external names (www.example.com, etc.) fails even though the network path is working, making browsers “timeout” and be perceived as Internet down.

Localization: srv1 (10.5.0.4) DNS daemon (named) on eth0 is answering but with recursion disabled. Mechanism: clients in Office A send DNS to srv1; srv1 refuses to recurse, so name resolution fails and applications cannot reach sites by name.

Root cause: a recent configuration change on srv1 disabled DNS recursion (authoritative-only), leaving clients without a recursive resolver.

Corrective fix: Re-enable recursion or configure forwarders in BIND on srv1, then restart named. For example, in /etc/bind/named.conf set “recursion yes;” and optionally “allow-recursion { 10.0.0.0/8; };” and, if desired, “forwarders { <upstream DNS IPs>; };” Then run: systemctl restart bind9. Alternatively, temporarily point clients to a working recursive DNS. No WAN/router restart is needed.
=== END ===